  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}.\n$$",
